            "SAME MOMENT ON THE OTHER BANDS (dB)",
            "Milky Way",
            "worst",
            "current · new",
            "Tant and Te taken from each band's EME budget.",
            "WHY IT CHANGES WITH FREQUENCY",
            "Sky noise drops with frequency (index 2.6): at 50 MHz it is thousands of kelvin, at 432 MHz a few tens.",
            "How much it counts depends on the station's own noise: with real Tant and Te, not with the same 80 K for everyone.",
            "The antenna beam widens or narrows the patch of sky that enters the calculation.",
            "The DX has their own Dgrd, calculated with their station.",
            "DGRD by frequency",
            "The noise model (receiver and sky) is chosen in the DGRD by frequency tab.",
            "Dgrd at your working frequency, compared with the usual calculation (80 K receiver, sky only at the Moon's position): you can use your station's real noise (Tant and Te from the band's EME budget) and the sky averaged over the antenna beam. On the right, how the Dgrd of the selected day builds up and the same moment on the other bands. The choices apply throughout the program, including the best days in Distance and Dgrd.",
            "Link budget for each chosen band (50 to 1296 MHz), with the data of each band stored separately. On the left the two stations (antenna and gain, BETA for antennas without elevation), six indicators (QSO possible, my EIRP and the DX's, path loss, S/N me → DX and DX → me in the chosen bandwidth and with the Sun, also referred to 2500 Hz) and the table with all the steps; on the right the diagram of the transmit and receive system of the chosen station: antenna and Tant, antenna – relay cable, coaxial relay, transmitter with its cable, preamplifier, downlead cable, optional transverter, cables and radio with MDS and mode. The cables are chosen from the database of the COAXIAL CABLES tab and the losses are calculated automatically. At the top right the IN-DEPTH CALCULATIONS box, the same as on the main screen.",
            "Polar (equatorial)",
            "single stage",
            "two stages",
            "Dish diameter in metres.",
            "Aperture efficiency of the dish with its feed: 60% is the average value, 60–80% the typical range (PE1RKI).",
            "Ratio of focal length to diameter: used to choose the feed; the focus is at f/D × D from the vertex.",
            "Dish mount: azimuth-elevation or polar (equatorial). For now it is only a note and does not enter the calculation.",
            "Single- or two-stage preamplifier: with two stages the total gain reaches about 30 dB and the noise of cable and transverter becomes negligible.",
            "Transverter output power in transmit (W): it drives the amplifier.",
            "Drive power the amplifier needs to deliver the set output power (W).",
            "Cable between the transverter TX output and the amplifier input.",
            "Length of the cable between transverter and amplifier.",
            "Dish diameter",
            "Efficiency (feed + dish)",
            "f/D ratio",
            "Mount",
            "typical 60–80%",
            "→ focus {0:F2} m from vertex",
            "Stages",
            "Required drive",
            "TX output",
            "Cable to PA",
            "dish {0:F2} m",
            "TX: {0} → cable {1:F2} dB → at PA {2}",
            "insufficient drive ({0} needed)",
            "drive sufficient",
            "Gain = 10·log[η·(π·D/λ)²] (PE1RKI)",
            "{0:F1} dBi ({1:F1} dBd) · −3 dB beamwidth ≈ 70·λ/D = {2:F1}°",
            "EME budget Yagi",
            "EME budget dishes",
            "The same calculation as the EME budget Yagi, for stations with a dish antenna, at 432, 902 and 1296 MHz, with values stored separately. In each station's tab you enter diameter, efficiency (60% on average, 60–80% typical) and f/D ratio; the gain is calculated with the PE1RKI formula, G = 10·log[η·(π·D/λ)²], with dBd (dBi − 2.15) and the −3 dB beamwidth (70·λ/D). The diagram shows the dish with its feed and the complete chain: single- or two-stage preamplifier (up to about 30 dB), feed line, transverter with its transmit output and the cable to the amplifier, with a check of the drive power required by the PA.",
            "Try moving me left or right",
            "Data sheets by Times Microwave, RFS, CommScope Andrew, Siva Cavi, SSB-Electronic, Messi & Paoloni (via WiMo), Symair, SYV50-7 and the table by SV1BSX; values brought to the bands with the formula α = k1·√f + k2·f.",
            "≈ = computed with the formula α = k1·√f + k2·f (conductors + dielectric) within the frequencies of the data sheet.",
            "e = computed with the same formula outside the frequencies of the data sheet: to be used with caution.",
            "for the development news",
            "on the frequencies above 432 MHz",
            "WSJT-X guide (K1JT and the WSJT Development Group), § 7.1: JT65 −25 dB, JT9 −27 dB, JT4 −23 dB, FT8 about −20 dB, FT4 3.5 dB less sensitive than FT8. Q65: K1JT, G4WJS, K9AN and IV3NWV, “Quick-Start Guide to Q65” (3 April 2021), 50 % decode threshold without AP: Q65-30A −24.8 dB, Q65-60A −27.6 dB, Q65-120A −30.8 dB. For Q65-60B and Q65-60C the guide gives no threshold: YAEC uses the Q65-60A one as an estimate. All values are referred to 2500 Hz, without fading or Doppler spread (AWGN channel).",
            "Mode used and its typical decoding threshold (S/N in 2500 Hz, WSJT-X guide), shown as a reference only: with Deep Search, message averaging or AP decoding you can decode even lower (see the manual). The thresholds apply without fading or Doppler spread: with EME spread they get worse.",
            "Estimated number of connectors in the transmit line, between the PA and the antenna, including adapters, relays and splitters. NJ9R's advice: about 1 dB for every 8 – 10 connectors, i.e. 0.11 dB each. The rule comes from 144 MHz: YAEC applies it to all bands.",
            "For antennas fixed on the horizon: adds the gain due to ground reflection (up to +6 dB in theory; IK1UWL's measurements with a high antenna give about 3 dB).",
            "The RSTN network measures the Sun from 245 to 15400 MHz: the flux at 50 – 432 MHz is derived from the lowest measurements, the flux at 902 and 1296 MHz is interpolated between 610 and 1415 MHz. With F10.7 alone (2800 MHz) the flux at low frequencies is estimated in proportion to a typical quiet Sun: it is a rough estimate, to be compared with the manual value.",
            "EXPERIMENTAL. Flux at the working frequency: 902 and 1296 MHz interpolated between 610 and 1415 MHz, 432 MHz interpolated between 410 and 610 MHz, 222, 144, 70 and 50 MHz extrapolated from 245 and 410 MHz with a power law (the most uncertain part: compare with the manual value). Sun treated as an unpolarized point source (the antenna collects half of it): T = S·Ae/(2k), with Ae from the total gain, attenuated by the pattern (Gaussian lobe with width estimated from the gain, then side lobes at the set level). Sun below the receiver horizon: no contribution. RSTN measurements are local noon values and do not follow current bursts.",
            "The bands used in calculations and lists, from 50 MHz to 23 cm (1296 – 1298 MHz).",
            "Database of 50 Ω coaxial cables with the attenuation in dB/100 m in the seven bands from 50 to 1296 MHz (datasheet values and the formula α = k1·√f + k2·f), merged cables and equivalent names, comparison of the ticked cables and loss over your own length; the chosen cables are used in the system diagram of the EME budget.",
            "The database of 50 Ω coaxial cables with the attenuation at 50, 70, 144, 222, 432, 902 and 1296 MHz (datasheet values and the formula α = k1·√f + k2·f) and the source of every value; it sorts by diameter, loss, name, manufacturer or type (also by clicking the column headers), filters by type and diameter and searches by name. On the right, the loss of a run and the chart of the ticked cables; the \"use in EME Budget\" button takes the cable to the Budget. Custom cables can be added.",
            "The tabs are gathered in six groups in the bar at the top: Live (Live position and +- 24h, Reconnaissance of my obstacles, My horizon, Sky view), Planning (Distance and Dgrd, DGRD by frequency, Stations to try, Moon position, Moon over the world), Link (EME budget Yagi, EME budget dishes, GRAVES radar, Solar noise, Export calculations to Excel, Calculation check), Log (WWL map and ADIF), Help and tools (USER MANUAL, THEORY AND TIPS ON EME, COAXIAL CABLES), Credits and thanks (ABOUT & CREDITS, AUTHOR CONTACTS AND ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS, LICENCE). Click a group in the first row and then the tab in the second; each group remembers the last tab opened. The grey tabs are enabled with the \"IN-DEPTH CALCULATIONS\" box.",
            "1.6 - 902 MHz and 23 cm (1296 and 1298 MHz) bands throughout the program, with all the bands in the MHz drop-down list; the EME budget tab becomes EME budget Yagi and there is the new EME budget dishes tab (prime-focus dish, gain with the PE1RKI formula, preamplifier also with two stages, transverter and PA drive); new DGRD by frequency tab, with the real noise of the station and the sky averaged over the beam, used throughout the program; coaxial cables calculated with the formula α = k1·√f + k2·f from the datasheet data, also at 902 and 1296 MHz; new style of the controls in all tabs; in Live position and +- 24h, the tooltip on the red cursor and the red DX lines at the cursor time; manual revised after comparing the sources (path loss band by band, thresholds of Q65-60B and Q65-60C, ground gain according to IK1UWL's measurements, connectors, Dgrd of other programs, dishes above 432 MHz); packages for every architecture (Windows x64 and x86, Linux x64, arm64 and arm, macOS Apple Silicon and Intel); thanks to Robert HA5OLA. The bands above 432 MHz are still being completed.",
            "echo calculation",
            "I transmit and receive my own echo: the DX column disappears and the budget uses my station for both transmission and reception, with the Moon distance from my station on the way out and on the way back.",
