            "Show the best",
            "S/N in both directions at the best moment of each common window: power, losses, gains, EME path, Dgrd, polarisation and noise in 2500 Hz.",
            "Margin = S/N of the weaker direction − threshold of the mode.",
            "Index = 50 + 5 × margin, from 0 to 100: margin 0 dB = 50, +10 dB = 100, −10 dB = 0.",
            "Windows shorter than one hour weigh in proportion to their duration.",
            "With the temperatures box: Tsys = Tant + sky behind the Moon + Te, local noise from the Local noise map (NJ9R). Without: reference noise and WSJT-X Dgrd.",
            "HOW THE INDEX IS COMPUTED",
            "DL6WU spacings (W4RNL): reflector 0.2 λ, first director 0.075 λ, then growing up to 0.4 λ. G = 7.773·log(L/λ) + 11.43 (W8IO). Below 2 λ of boom the value is indicative; non-DL6WU antennas may differ.",
            "FROM ELEMENTS TO GAIN (DL6WU, 144 MHz)",
            "Elements",
            "Boom (m)",
            "Gain (dBi)",
            "My horizon",
            "Stations to try",
            "for the idea of the tab",
            "Two views. Horizon from terrain: the panorama of the terrain around your station, computed from the SRTM1 data (about 30 m) downloaded only once into data\\dem, with the curvature of the Earth and refraction; in the background the sky dome with the noise sources (three colour scales, as in the Sky view) or a light blue sky, the path of the Moon with rise and set over the terrain, the main obstacles with names taken from OpenStreetMap and the minutes of Moon lost. With the box «Use terrain obstructions in the calculations» the minimum elevation follows the terrain in the whole program. Local noise map (NJ9R): an azimuth × elevation grid in which you note with the keys 1 to 10 the noise you see in WSJT-X while rotating the antenna; the hours with the Moon in a noisy direction have the ⚠ sign in the calendar and in the Sky view. With the «My station / DX» selector the same view shows the DX horizon, computed around its locator with the antenna at the chosen height above ground; with the «Terrain obstructions for the DX too» box its terrain enters the common windows, the 24-hour bar, the Sky view and Stations to try.",
            "For the chosen day, the stations active in the N0UK EME chat (144 MHz only) with which you have a common window, the best one for each locator field, sorted by an index from 0 to 100 derived from the S/N in both directions at the best moment of each window. The DX setup is reconstructed from what is written in the chat (antennas, elements, power), yours comes from the EME budget. It is an example calculation, very approximate: compare it with the QSOs you actually make. Idea by Matteo IW5DHN.",
            "Tile {0} ({1} of {2})…",
            "Computing the horizon…",
            "sea",
            "gap towards {0}",
            "Blue",
            "Purple",
            "Green",
            "1.9 - New tab My horizon (Live): horizon from the terrain computed from SRTM1 data with Earth curvature and refraction, over the Sky view background in one of the three colour scales, Moon path, main obstacles with OpenStreetMap names and minutes of Moon lost; checkbox to use the terrain as minimum elevation throughout the program; local noise map after NJ9R, with ⚠ in the hours when the Moon is in a noisy direction; new tab Stations to try (Planning), idea by Matteo IW5DHN: the active stations of the N0UK chat ranked by an index from 0 to 100; numeric boxes with side-by-side arrows, pill-shaped tabs and three new colour schemes taken from IP-TRX-CTRL.",
            " days: {0}, Dgrd {1:F2} dB at {2} UTC (distance {3:N0} km), {4:0.0} useful hours.   Computed for {5} at {6} MHz (classic Dgrd, as in the EME tables){7}. Updated at {8} UTC.",
            "CORRECT",
            "Correct the locator in CALL3.TXT",
            "The locator you typed differs from the one in CALL3.TXT: one click saves it as a correction. YAEC keeps it in data\\call3_correzioni.txt, applies it to CALL3.TXT at once and again at every update of the file, including the copy for WSJT-X.",
            "Change the locator of {0} in CALL3.TXT from {1} to {2}?",
            "The correction stays valid after CALL3.TXT updates too.",
