            "John C. Sager G8ONH, with John Regnault G4SWX, \"Beyond DJ9BV – A high-resolution antenna temperature calculator 'Skynoise'\" (2026) and the Skynoise 0.2.2 program (MIT licence, text below): method rewritten in C# for YAEC; the Remazeilles map at the bands is derived from its SkyTemp144.fits file. HEALPix bilinear interpolation (K. M. Górski et al., ApJ 622, 759, 2005).",
            "Sky maps for Sky noise",
            "C. G. T. Haslam et al., A&AS 47, 1 (1982), 408 MHz map from LAMBDA (NASA); M. Remazeilles et al., MNRAS 451, 4311 (2015); E. Polisensky, LWA Memo 111 (2007), LFmap; extragalactic correction by P. A. G. Lawson et al., MNRAS 225, 307 (1987); atmosphere at 23 cm according to ITU-R P.676 and P.372, air mass by F. Kasten and A. T. Young (1989). Comparison method: R. Bertelsmeier DJ9BV, DUBUS 1987 and 1991.",
            "Antenna temperature computed with the Skynoise method (G8ONH, G4SWX): the full antenna pattern (NEC .out file, YA-NEC file or Gaussian beam of the EME budget) is pointed at the Moon and each direction reads the sky map at the chosen band (Remazeilles, Haslam, GSM 2008 or LFmap); below the horizon it is 290 K or the terrain of My horizon. It shows the map with the Moon path over the month (click a day for its pass), the antenna temperature during the pass for you and for the DX, the sky behind the Moon, the check average gain, the effect on the EME budget Yagi and the comparison with the Gaussian beam and with the DJ9BV method.",
            "1.9.2 - New tab Sky noise (Skynoise) in Planning: antenna temperature computed with the G8ONH and G4SWX method from the full antenna pattern (NEC .out file, YA-NEC or Gaussian beam) and the sky map (Remazeilles, Haslam, GSM 2008 or LFmap) at 50, 144, 432 MHz and 23 cm, with the Moon path over the month, the temperature during the pass for you and for the DX, the effect on the EME budget Yagi and the comparison with DJ9BV; automatic test in compila.bat; in the THEORY AND TIPS ON EME manual: sky noise with the sources compared, dish gain (PE1RKI or itemised calculation, Ruze), prime focus above 2320 MHz, feed rotation and the Moon illuminated by the beam (BWF).",
            "1.9.3 - Package names the same as YA-MS (-windows-x64/-x86/-arm64, -windows7-x64/-x86, -mac-apple-silicon, -mac-intel, -linux-x64/-arm64/-armv7l/-x86, -raspberry-pi-64bit/-32bit, -alpine-x64/-arm64); .rpm, Arch and Slackware packages for ARM too; the release notes have a table with a link to each package for each system. This version also contains the files with the old names, only for the automatic update from 1.9.1 and 1.9.2.",
            "Cost of 0.1 dB more loss ahead of the preamp (S/N and G/T)",
            "How much the illumination drops at the dish edge compared with the centre, including the space loss (dB): 10 – 12 dB gives the highest efficiency (W1GHZ). It is used to estimate the spillover, the feed power that goes past the rim and sees the warm ground.",
